= #text("The Ethics of Belief / 信念的伦理")

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("I. The Duty of Inquiry.—A shipowner was about to send to sea an emigrant-ship. He knew that she was old, and not over-well built at the first; that she had seen many seas and climes, and often had needed repairs. Doubts had been suggested to him that possibly she was not seaworthy. These doubts preyed upon his mind, and made him unhappy; he thought that perhaps he ought to have her thoroughly overhauled and refitted, even though this should put him to great expense. Before the ship sailed, however, he succeeded in overcoming these melancholy reflections. He said to himself that she had gone safely through so many voyages and weathered so many storms that it was idle to suppose she would not come safely home from this trip also. He would put his trust in Providence, which could hardly fail to protect all these unhappy families that were leaving their fatherland to seek for better times elsewhere. He would dismiss from his mind all ungenerous suspicions about the honesty of builders and contractors. In such ways he acquired a sincere and comfortable conviction that his vessel was thoroughly safe and seaworthy; he watched her departure with a light heart, and benevolent wishes for the success of the exiles in their strange new home that was to be; and he got his insurance-money when she went down in mid-ocean and told no tales.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("I. 调查义务——一位船东正要派遣一艘移民船出海。他知道她已经老了，而且身材一开始就不太好。她见过许多海洋和气候，并且经常需要修理。有人向他提出怀疑，认为她可能不适合航海。这些疑虑折磨着他的心灵，使他不高兴。他认为也许他应该对她进行彻底的检修和改装，尽管这会让他付出巨大的代价。然而，在船启航之前，他成功地克服了这些忧郁的想法。他对自己说，她已经安全地经历了那么多的航行，经历了那么多的风暴，所以认为她这次旅行也不会安全回家是毫无意义的。他相信普罗维登斯，它不可能不保护所有这些离开祖国到别处寻找更好生活的不幸家庭。他会排除所有对建筑商和承包商诚实度的不厚道的怀疑。通过这种方式，他获得了真诚而舒适的信念，相信他的船是完全安全且适航的。他怀着轻松的心情看着她离开，并衷心祝愿流放者在他们即将到来的陌生新家取得成功。当她沉入大海并且没有讲任何故事时，他得到了保险金。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("What shall we say of him? Surely this, that he was verily guilty of the death of those men. It is admitted that he did sincerely believe in the soundness of his ship; but the sincerity of his conviction can in no wise help him, because he had no right to believe on such evidence as was before him. He had acquired his belief not by honestly earning it in patient investigation, but by stifling his doubts. And although in the end he may have felt so sure about it that he could not think otherwise, yet inasmuch as he had knowingly and willingly worked himself into that frame of mind, he must be held responsible for it.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("我们该怎么评价他呢？毫无疑问，他确实对这些人的死负有责任。必须承认的是，他确实真诚地相信他的船是完好的。但他的信念的诚意对他没有任何帮助，因为他没有权利相信他面前的这些证据。他的信仰并不是通过耐心调查而诚实地获得的，而是通过压制自己的疑虑而获得的。尽管最终他可能对此深信不疑，无法做出其他想法，但由于他是故意且自愿地陷入这种心态，因此他必须对此负责。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("Let us alter the case a little, and suppose that the ship was not unsound after all; that she made her voyage safely, and many others after it. Will that diminish the guilt of her owner? Not one jot. When an action is once done, it is right or wrong forever; no accidental failure of its good or evil fruits can possibly alter that. The man would not have been innocent, he would only have been not found out. The question of right or wrong has to do with the origin of his belief, not the matter of it; not what it was, but how he got it; not whether it turned out to be true or false, but whether he had a right to believe on such evidence as was before him.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("让我们稍微改变一下情况，假设这艘船毕竟还不错。她和许多其他人都安全航行了。这会减轻她主人的罪责吗？没有一点。一个行为一旦做了，就永远是对或错；它的善果或恶果的意外失败不可能改变这一点。这个人不可能是无辜的，他只是不会被发现。对错的问题与他的信仰的起源有关，而不是它的问题；不在于它是什么，而在于他是如何得到它的；问题不在于它是真是假，而在于他是否有权相信他面前的这些证据。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("There was once an island in which some of the inhabitants professed a religion teaching neither the doctrine of original sin nor that of eternal punishment. A suspicion got abroad that the professors of this religion had made use of unfair means to get their doctrines taught to children. They were accused of wresting the laws of their country in such a way as to remove children from the care of their natural and legal guardians; and even of stealing them away and keeping them concealed from their friends and relations. A certain number of men formed themselves into a society for the purpose of agitating the public about this matter. They published grave accusations against individual citizens of the highest position and character, and did all in their power to injure these citizens in the exercise of their professions. So great was the noise they made, that a Commission was appointed to investigate the facts; but after the Commission had carefully inquired into all the evidence that could be got, it appeared that the accused were innocent. Not only had they been accused on insufficient evidence, but the evidence of their innocence was such as the agitators might easily have obtained, if they had attempted a fair inquiry. After these disclosures the inhabitants of that country looked upon the members of the agitating society, not only as persons whose judgment was to be distrusted, but also as no longer to be counted honorable men. For although they had sincerely and conscientiously believed in the charges they had made, yet they had no right to believe on such evidence as was before them. Their sincere convictions, instead of being honestly earned by patient inquiring, were stolen by listening to the voice of prejudice and passion.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("从前有一个岛屿，岛上的一些居民信奉一种宗教，既不教授原罪教义，也不教授永恒惩罚的教义。有人怀疑该宗教的教授使用不公平的手段向儿童传授其教义。他们被指控侵犯本国法律，使儿童脱离其自然和法定监护人的照顾；甚至偷走它们并向朋友和亲戚隐瞒它们。为了煽动公众关注此事，一定数量的人组成了一个社团。他们对具有最高地位和品格的个别公民发表严重指控，并竭尽全力伤害这些在履行职责的公民。他们制造的噪音如此之大，以至于任命了一个委员会来调查事实。但在委员会仔细调查了所有可以获得的证据后，被告似乎是无辜的。他们不仅被指控证据不足，而且如果煽动者试图进行公正的调查，他们很容易就能获得无罪的证据。这些事件曝光后，该国居民不仅将煽动社会的成员视为判断力不可信的人，而且不再将其视为可敬之人。因为虽然他们真诚地、认真地相信他们所提出的指控，但他们无权相信这样的证据：就在他们面前。他们的真诚信念不是通过耐心询问而诚实获得的，而是通过聆听偏见和激情的声音而被窃取的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("Let us vary this case also, and suppose, other things remaining as before, that a still more accurate investigation proved the accused to have been really guilty. Would this make any difference in the guilt of the accusers? Clearly not; the question is not whether their belief was true or false, but whether they entertained it on wrong grounds. They would no doubt say, ‘Now you see that we were right after all; next time perhaps you will believe us.’ And they might be believed, but they would not thereby become honorable men. They would not be innocent, they would only be not found out. Every one of them, if he chose to examine himself in foro conscientiæ, would know that he had acquired and nourished a belief, when he had no right to believe on such evidence as was before him; and therein he would know that he had done a wrong thing.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("让我们也改变这个案例，假设其他事情与以前一样，更准确的调查证明被告确实有罪。这会对原告的罪责产生任何影响吗？显然不是；问题不在于他们的信念是真是假，而在于他们的信念是否有错误。他们无疑会说：‘现在你知道我们终究是对的；下次也许你会相信我们。’他们可能会被相信，但他们不会因此而成为正人君子。他们不会是无辜的，只是不会被发现。他们中的每一个人，如果他选择以良心的方式审视自己，就会知道他已经获得并培育了一种信仰，而他无权相信眼前的证据；从此他就会知道自己做错了事。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("It may be said, however, that in both of these supposed cases it is not the belief which is judged to be wrong, but the action following upon it. The shipowner might say, ‘I am perfectly certain that my ship is sound, but still I feel it my duty to have her examined, before trusting the lives of so many people to her.’ And it might be said to the agitator, ‘However convinced you were of the justice of your cause and the truth of your convictions, you ought not to have made a public attack upon any man’s character until you had examined the evidence on both sides with the utmost patience and care.’")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("然而，可以说，在这两种假设的情况下，被判断为错误的不是信念，而是随之而来的行动。船东可能会说，“我完全确定我的船是完好的，但在把这么多人的生命托付给她之前，我仍然觉得有责任对她进行检查。”也可能对煽动者说，“无论你相信你的事业是正义的，你的信念是真实的，你不应该公开攻击任何人的品格，除非你以最大的耐心和谨慎检查了双方的证据。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("In the first place, let us admit that, so far as it goes, this view of the case is right and necessary; right, because even when a man’s belief is so fixed that he cannot think otherwise, he still has a choice in regard to the action suggested by it, and so cannot escape the duty of investigating on the ground of the strength of his convictions; and necessary, because those who are not yet capable of controlling their feelings and thoughts must have a plain rule dealing with overt acts.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("首先，我们必须承认，就目前的情况来看，这种观点是正确的、必要的。是的，因为即使一个人的信念如此固定，以至于他不能有不同的想法，他仍然可以选择它所暗示的行动，因此不能逃避根据其信念的强度进行调查的义务；而且是必要的，因为那些还没有能力控制自己的感情和思想的人必须有一个明确的规则来处理公开的行为。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("But this being premised as necessary, it becomes clear that it is not sufficient, and that our previous judgment is required to supplement it. For it is not possible so to sever the belief from the action it suggests as to condemn the one without condemning the other. No man holding a strong belief on one side of a question, or even wishing to hold a belief on one side, can investigate it with such fairness and completeness as if he were really in doubt and unbiased; so that the existence of a belief not founded on fair inquiry unfits a man for the performance of this necessary duty.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("但以必要为前提，显然还不够，还需要我们之前的判断来补充。因为不可能将信念与其所暗示的行动割裂开来，从而谴责一个人而不谴责另一个人。没有人对一个问题抱有坚定的信念，或者甚至希望坚持某一方面的信念，能够以如此公平和完整的方式调查它，就好像他真的有怀疑和不带偏见一样；因此，如果一个人的信仰不是建立在公平调查的基础上，那么他就无法履行这一必要的职责。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("Nor is that truly a belief at all which has not some influence upon the actions of him who holds it. He who truly believes that which prompts him to an action has looked upon the action to lust after it, he has committed it already in his heart. If a belief is not realized immediately in open deeds, it is stored up for the guidance of the future. It goes to make a part of that aggregate of beliefs which is the link between sensation and action at every moment of all our lives, and which is so organized and compacted together that no part of it can be isolated from the rest, but every new addition modifies the structure of the whole. No real belief, however trifling and fragmentary it may seem, is ever truly insignificant; it prepares us to receive more of its like, confirms those which resembled it before, and weakens others; and so gradually it lays a stealthy train in our inmost thoughts, which may some day explode into overt action, and leave its stamp upon our character forever.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("如果这种信念对持有该信念的人的行为没有任何影响，那么它根本就不是真正的信念。真正相信促使他采取某项行动的人，已经看到了该行动并对其产生了欲望，他已经在心里犯下了它。如果一个信念没有立即在公开的行动中实现，它就会被储存起来以供未来的指导。它成为信念集合体的一部分，而信念集合体是我们一生中每时每刻的感觉和行动之间的联系，它是如此组织和紧凑在一起，以至于它的任何部分都不能与其余部分分离，但每一个新的添加都会改变整体的结构。任何真正的信仰，无论看起来多么微不足道、支离破碎，都不是真正微不足道的。它使我们准备好接受更多类似的东西，确认那些以前与它相似的东西，并削弱其他的东西；就这样，它渐渐地在我们内心深处埋下了一条隐秘的列车，有一天可能会爆发为公开的行动，并永远在我们的性格中留下印记。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("And no one man’s belief is in any case a private matter which concerns himself alone. Our lives are guided by that general conception of the course of things which has been created by society for social purposes. Our words, our phrases, our forms and processes and modes of thought, are common property, fashioned and perfected from age to age; an heirloom which every succeeding generation inherits as a precious deposit and a sacred trust to be handed on to the next one, not unchanged but enlarged and purified, with some clear marks of its proper handiwork. Into this, for good or ill, is woven every belief of every man who has speech of his fellows. An awful privilege, and an awful responsibility, that we should help to create the world in which posterity will live.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("无论如何，任何人的信仰都不是一件只与他个人有关的私人事务。我们的生活受到社会出于社会目的而创造的事物进程的一般概念的指导。我们的言语、短语、形式、过程和思维方式都是共同财产，随着时代的发展而不断形成和完善；这是每一代人都继承的传家宝，作为珍贵的存款和神圣的信托，传递给下一代，不是一成不变，而是扩大和纯化，并带有一些明显的手工制作痕迹。无论好坏，每个拥有同胞言论的人的每一个信念都与此交织在一起。我们应该帮助创造一个让子孙后代生活在其中的世界，这是一项可怕的特权，也是一项可怕的责任。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("In the two supposed cases which have been considered, it has been judged wrong to believe on insufficient evidence, or to nourish belief by suppressing doubts and avoiding investigation. The reason of this judgment is not far to seek: it is that in both these cases the belief held by one man was of great importance to other men. But forasmuch as no belief held by one man, however seemingly trivial the belief, and however obscure the believer, is ever actually insignificant or without its effect on the fate of mankind, we have no choice but to extend our judgment to all cases of belief whatever. Belief, that sacred faculty which prompts the decisions of our will, and knits into harmonious working all the compacted energies of our being, is ours not for ourselves, but for humanity. It is rightly used on truths which have been established by long experience and waiting toil, and which have stood in the fierce light of free and fearless questioning. Then it helps to bind men together, and to strengthen and direct their common action. It is desecrated when given to unproved and unquestioned statements, for the solace and private pleasure of the believer; to add a tinsel splendor to the plain straight road of our life and display a bright mirage beyond it; or even to drown the common sorrows of our kind by a self-deception which allows them not only to cast down, but also to degrade us. Whoso would deserve well of his fellows in this matter will guard the purity of his belief with a very fanaticism of jealous care, lest at any time it should rest on an unworthy object, and catch a stain which can never be wiped away.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("在我们所考虑的两个假设案例中，在证据不足的情况下相信，或者通过压制怀疑和逃避调查来滋养信念，都被认为是错误的。这一判断的原因不难找到：在这两种情况下，一个人所持有的信念对其他人来说非常重要。但是，由于一个人持有的任何信仰，无论看起来多么微不足道，无论信仰者多么默默无闻，实际上都是微不足道的，或者对人类的命运没有影响，所以我们别无选择，只能将我们的判断延伸到所有信仰的案例。信仰，这种神圣的能力，它促使我们做出意志的决定，并将我们存在的所有压缩能量融为一体，和谐地工作，它不是为了我们自己，而是为了人类。它正确地用于那些通过长期经验和等待的辛劳而建立起来的真理，这些真理已经站在自由和无畏质疑的强烈光芒下。然后，它有助于将人们团结在一起，并加强和指导他们的共同行动。当为了信徒的安慰和私人快乐而给予未经证实和未经质疑的陈述时，它就被亵渎了；为我们平淡笔直的人生之路增添一丝光彩，并在其后展现出灿烂的海市蜃楼；或者甚至用自欺欺人的方式来淹没我们同类的共同悲伤，这不仅让他们感到沮丧，而且还贬低了我们。凡是在这件事上值得他的同伴们尊敬的人，都会以一种非常狂热和嫉妒的态度来捍卫自己信仰的纯洁性，以免任何时候它会落在一个不值得的物体上，并被抓住。永远无法抹去的污点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("It is not only the leader of men, statesman, philosopher, or poet, that owes this bounden duty to mankind. Every rustic who delivers in the village alehouse his slow, infrequent sentences, may help to kill or keep alive the fatal superstitions which clog his race. Every hard-worked wife of an artisan may transmit to her children beliefs which shall knit society together, or rend it in pieces. No simplicity of mind, no obscurity of station, can escape the universal duty of questioning all that we believe.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("不仅是人类领袖、政治家、哲学家或诗人对人类负有这一义不容辞的责任。每一个乡下人在村里的酒馆里缓慢地、不频繁地发出句子，都可能有助于消灭或保持阻碍他的种族的致命迷信。每一位勤劳的工匠妻子都可以向她的孩子传递信仰，这些信仰将使社会团结在一起，或者将社会撕裂。任何简单的头脑、任何默默无闻的立场，都无法逃脱质疑我们所相信的一切的普遍责任。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("It is true that this duty is a hard one, and the doubt which comes out of it is often a very bitter thing. It leaves us bare and powerless where we thought that we were safe and strong. To know all about anything is to know how to deal with it under all circumstances. We feel much happier and more secure when we think we know precisely what to do, no matter what happens, than when we have lost our way and do not know where to turn. And if we have supposed ourselves to know all about anything, and to be capable of doing what is fit in regard to it, we naturally do not like to find that we are really ignorant and powerless, that we have to begin again at the beginning, and try to learn what the thing is and how it is to be dealt with—if indeed anything can be learnt about it. It is the sense of power attached to a sense of knowledge that makes men desirous of believing, and afraid of doubting.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("确实，这项责任是一项艰巨的任务，而由此产生的怀疑往往是一件非常痛苦的事情。当我们自以为安全、坚强的时候，它却让我们赤裸裸、无能为力。了解一切就是知道在任何情况下如何处理它。当我们认为无论发生什么，我们都清楚地知道该做什么时，我们会感到比迷失方向、不知道该转向哪里时更快乐、更安全。如果我们认为自己对任何事情都了解，并且有能力做适合它的事情，那么我们自然不喜欢发现自己真的无知和无能为力，我们必须从头开始，尝试了解事情是什么以及如何处理它——如果确实可以学到任何东西的话。正是知识感所附带的权力感，使人们渴望相信，而害怕怀疑。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("This sense of power is the highest and best of pleasures when the belief on which it is founded is a true belief, and has been fairly earned by investigation. For then we may justly feel that it is common property, and holds good for others as well as for ourselves. Then we may be glad, not that I have learned secrets by which I am safer and stronger, but that we men have got mastery over more of the world; and we shall be strong, not for ourselves, but in the name of Man and in his strength. But if the belief has been accepted on insufficient evidence, the pleasure is a stolen one. Not only does it deceive ourselves by giving us a sense of power which we do not really possess, but it is sinful, because it is stolen in defiance of our duty to mankind. That duty is to guard ourselves from such beliefs as from a pestilence, which may shortly master our own body and then spread to the rest of the town. What would be thought of one who, for the sake of a sweet fruit, should deliberately run the risk of bringing a plague upon his family and his neighbors?")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("当这种权力感所依据的信念是真正的信念，并且是通过调查而获得的时，这种权力感就是最高和最好的快乐。因为那时我们可能会合理地认为它是公共财产，对他人和我们自己都有好处。那么我们可能会感到高兴，不是因为我学到了使我更安全、更强大的秘密，而是因为我们人类已经掌握了世界的更多领域；我们将变得坚强，不是为了我们自己，而是以人类的名义和他的力量。但如果这种信念在证据不足的情况下被接受，那么这种快乐就是偷来的。它不仅通过给我们一种我们并不真正拥有的权力感来欺骗我们自己，而且它是有罪的，因为它被偷走了，无视我们对人类的责任。我们的责任就是保护自己免受瘟疫等信念的侵害，这种瘟疫可能很快就会控制我们的身体，然后传播到城镇的其他地方。如果一个人为了一颗甜果而故意冒着给家人和邻居带来瘟疫的风险，他会怎么想？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("And, as in other such cases, it is not the risk only which has to be considered; for a bad action is always bad at the time when it is done, no matter what happens afterward. Every time we let ourselves believe for unworthy reasons, we weaken our powers of self-control, of doubting, of judicially and fairly weighing evidence. We all suffer severely enough from the maintenance and support of false beliefs and the fatally wrong actions which they lead to, and the evil born when one such belief is entertained is great and wide. But a greater and wider evil arises when the credulous character is maintained and supported, when a habit of believing for unworthy reasons is fostered and made permanent. If I steal money from any person, there may be no harm done by the mere transfer of possession; he may not feel the loss, or it may prevent him from using the money badly. But I cannot help doing this great wrong toward Man, that I make myself dishonest. What hurts society is not that it should lose its property, but that it should become a den of thieves; for then it must cease to be society. This is why we ought not to do evil that good may come; for at any rate this great evil has come, that we have done evil and are made wicked thereby. In like manner, if I let myself believe anything on insufficient evidence, there may be no great harm done by the mere belief; it may be true after all, or I may never have occasion to exhibit it in outward acts. But I cannot help doing this great wrong toward Man, that I make myself credulous. The danger to society is not merely that it should believe wrong things, though that is great enough; but that it should become credulous, and lose the habit of testing things and inquiring into them; for then it must sink back into savagery.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("而且，与其他此类情况一样，不仅需要考虑风险，还需要考虑风险。因为坏的行为在做的时候总是坏的，无论之后发生什么。每当我们让自己相信一些不值得的理由时，我们就会削弱我们的自我控制、怀疑、司法和公平衡量证据的能力。我们所有人都因错误信念的维持和支持以及它们所导致的致命错误行为而遭受足够严重的痛苦，而当怀有这样一种信念时，所产生的邪恶是巨大而广泛的。但是，当轻信的性格得到维持和支持，当出于不值得的理由而相信的习惯被养成并永久化时，更大和更广泛的邪恶就会出现。如果我从任何人那里偷钱，仅仅转移所有权可能不会造成任何伤害；他可能感觉不到损失，或者可能会阻止他滥用这笔钱。但我情不自禁地对人类做出了巨大的错误，我让自己变得不诚实。伤害社会的不是它失去财产，而是它成为贼窝；因为那时它就不再是社会了。这就是为什么我们不应该为善而行恶；无论如何，这个大祸已经来临，我们做了恶事，并因此而变得邪恶。同样地，如果我让自己在证据不足的情况下相信任何事情，仅仅相信这一点可能不会造成太大的伤害；这可能毕竟是真的，或者我可能永远没有机会在外在行为中表现出来。但我情不自禁地对人类做出了这样的大错，我让自己轻信了。对社会的危险不仅仅是它应该相信错误的事情，尽管这已经足够了；但它应该变得轻信，并且失去测试和探究事物的习惯；因为到那时它就必须重新陷入野蛮状态。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("The harm which is done by credulity in a man is not confined to the fostering of a credulous character in others, and consequent support of false beliefs. Habitual want of care about what I believe leads to habitual want of care in others about the truth of what is told to me. Men speak the truth to one another when each reveres the truth in his own mind and in the other’s mind; but how shall my friend revere the truth in my mind when I myself am careless about it, when I believe things because I want to believe them, and because they are comforting and pleasant? Will he not learn to cry, ‘Peace,’ to me, when there is no peace? By such a course I shall surround myself with a thick atmosphere of falsehood and fraud, and in that I must live. It may matter little to me, in my cloud-castle of sweet illusions and darling lies; but it matters much to Man that I have made my neighbors ready to deceive. The credulous man is father to the liar and the cheat; he lives in the bosom of this his family, and it is no marvel if he should become even as they are. So closely are our duties knit together, that whoso shall keep the whole law, and yet offend in one point, he is guilty of all.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("轻信对一个人造成的伤害并不仅限于培养他人的轻信性格，以及随之而来的对错误信念的支持。习惯性地不关心我所相信的事情，会导致别人习惯性地不关心别人告诉我的事情的真相。当人们尊重自己和对方心中的真理时，他们就会彼此说真话。但是，当我自己对真理漠不关心时，当我相信事物是因为我愿意相信它们，并且因为它们令人安慰和愉快时，我的朋友怎么会尊重我心中的真理呢？当没有平安的时候，他难道不会学会向我呼喊“平安”吗？如果采取这样的做法，我将把自己包围在一种浓厚的虚假和欺诈的气氛中，而我必须生活在其中。在我甜蜜的幻想和可爱的谎言的云堡里，这对我来说可能无关紧要；但对人类来说，重要的是我让我的邻居做好了欺骗的准备。轻信的人是说谎者和骗子的父亲；他生活在这个家庭的怀抱中，如果他变得和他们一样，也就不足为奇了。我们的职责如此紧密地结合在一起，谁遵守了全部法律，却犯了某一点，他就犯了所有的罪。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("To sum up: it is wrong always, everywhere and for any one, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("总而言之：无论何时何地，对任何人来说，在证据不足的情况下相信任何事情都是错误的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("If a man, holding a belief which he was taught in childhood or persuaded of afterward, keeps down and pushes away any doubts which arise about it in his mind, purposely avoids the reading of books and the company of men that call in question or discuss it, and regards as impious those questions which cannot easily be asked without disturbing it—the life of that man is one long sin against mankind.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("如果一个人持有童年时被教导的或后来被说服的信念，压抑并消除心中出现的任何怀疑，故意避免阅读书籍，避免与质疑或讨论它的人交往，并认为那些在不干扰它的情况下不容易提出的问题是不虔诚的，那么这个人的生命就是一项对人类的长期罪恶。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("If this judgment seems harsh when applied to those simple souls who have never known better, who have been brought up from the cradle with a horror of doubt, and taught that their eternal welfare depends on what they believe, then it leads to the very serious question, Who hath made Israel to sin?")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("如果这种判断在应用于那些从未有过更好认识的简单灵魂时显得很严厉，他们从摇篮中就带着怀疑的恐惧长大，并被教导他们永恒的福祉取决于他们所相信的东西，那么这就引出了一个非常严重的问题，谁让以色列犯罪了？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("It may be permitted me to fortify this judgment with the sentence of Milton—")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("也许可以允许我用米尔顿的一句话来强化这一判断——")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("‘A man may be a heretic in the truth; and if he believe things only because his pastor says so, or the assembly so determine, without knowing other reason, though his belief be true, yet the very truth he holds becomes his heresy.’")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("‘一个人可能在真理上是异端；如果他仅仅因为他的牧师这样说，或者议会如此决定而相信某事，而不知道其他原因，那么尽管他的信仰是真实的，但他所持有的真理本身就成为他的异端。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("And with this famous aphorism of Coleridge—")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("用柯勒律治的这句著名格言来说——")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("‘He who begins by loving Christianity better than Truth, will proceed by loving his own sect or Church better than Christianity, and end in loving himself better than all.’")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("“谁一开始爱基督教胜过爱真理，他就会爱自己的教派或教会胜过爱基督教，最后爱自己胜过爱所有人。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("Inquiry into the evidence of a doctrine is not to be made once for all, and then taken as finally settled. It is never lawful to stifle a doubt; for either it can be honestly answered by means of the inquiry already made, or else it proves that the inquiry was not complete.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("对一个学说的证据的探究并不是一劳永逸的，然后就被认为是最终解决的。压制怀疑永远是不合法的；因为要么可以通过已经提出的询问诚实地回答，要么证明询问不完整。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("‘But,’ says one, ‘I am a busy man; I have no time for the long course of study which would be necessary to make me in any degree a competent judge of certain questions, or even able to understand the nature of the arguments.’ Then he should have no time to believe.")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("“但是，”一位人士说道，“我是个大忙人；我没有时间进行长期的学习，而这对于使我在任何程度上成为某些问题的合格判断，甚至能够理解论点的本质是必要的。”那么他就没有时间去相信。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("II. The Weight of Authority.—Are we then to become universal skeptics, doubting everything, afraid always to put one foot before the other until we have personally tested the firmness of the road? Are we to deprive ourselves of the help and guidance of that vast body of knowledge which is daily growing upon the world, because neither we nor any other one person can possibly test a hundredth part of it by immediate experiment or observation, and because it would not be completely proved if we did? Shall we steal and tell lies because we have had no personal experience wide enough to justify the belief that it is wrong to do so?")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("二.权威的重量——我们是否要成为普遍的怀疑论者，怀疑一切，害怕总是把一只脚放在另一只脚之前，直到我们亲自测试了道路的坚固性？我们是否应该剥夺自己对世界上日益增长的庞大知识体系的帮助和指导，因为我们或任何其他人都不可能通过直接的实验或观察来测试其中的百分之一，并且因为如果我们这样做，它也不会被完全证明？我们是否应该因为没有足够丰富的个人经验而相信这样做是错误的，就去偷窃和撒谎？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("There is no practical danger that such consequences will ever follow from scrupulous care and self-control in the matter of belief. Those men who have most nearly done their duty in this respect have found that certain great principles, and these most fitted for the guidance of life, have stood out more and more clearly in proportion to the care and honesty with which they were tested, and have acquired in this way a practical certainty. The beliefs about right and wrong which guide our actions in dealing with men in society, and the beliefs about physical nature which guide our actions in dealing with animate and inanimate bodies, these never suffer from investigation; they can take care of themselves, without being propped up by ‘acts of faith,’ the clamor of paid advocates, or the suppression of contrary evidence. Moreover there are many cases in which it is our duty to act upon probabilities, although the evidence is not such as to justify present belief; because it is precisely by such action, and by observation of its fruits, that evidence is got which may justify future belief. So that we have no reason to fear lest a habit of conscientious inquiry should paralyze the actions of our daily life.")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("如果在信仰问题上谨慎小心和自我控制，不会产生这种后果的实际危险。那些在这方面几乎履行了自己职责的人发现，某些伟大的原则，以及最适合指导生活的原则，随着他们经受考验的谨慎和诚实而变得越来越清晰，并以这种方式获得了实际的确定性。关于是非的信念指导我们在社会上与人打交道时的行为，以及关于物理性质的信念指导我们与有生命和无生命的物体打交道时的行为，这些信念从未受到调查；他们可以照顾自己，而无需受到“信仰行为”、付费倡导者的喧嚣或压制相反证据的支持。此外，在许多情况下，我们有责任根据概率采取行动，尽管证据并不能证明目前的信念是正确的。因为正是通过这样的行动，并通过对其成果的观察，才能获得证据，从而证明未来的信念是正确的。因此，我们没有理由担心认真探究的习惯会瘫痪我们的日常生活行为。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("But because it is not enough to say, ‘It is wrong to believe on unworthy evidence,’ without saying also what evidence is worthy, we shall now go on to inquire under what circumstances it is lawful to believe on the testimony of others; and then, further, we shall inquire more generally when and why we may believe that which goes beyond our own experience, or even beyond the experience of mankind.")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("但是，仅仅说“相信不值得的证据是错误的”是不够的，而不说什么证据是有价值的，我们现在要继续探讨在什么情况下相信他人的证词是合法的；然后，进一步，我们将更普遍地询问何时以及为什么我们可以相信超出我们自己的经验，甚至超出人类经验的东西。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("In what cases, then, let us ask in the first place, is the testimony of a man unworthy of belief? He may say that which is untrue either knowingly or unknowingly. In the first case he is lying, and his moral character is to blame; in the second case he is ignorant or mistaken, and it is only his knowledge or his judgment which is in fault. In order that we may have the right to accept his testimony as ground for believing what he says, we must have reasonable grounds for trusting his veracity, that he is really trying to speak the truth so far as he knows it; his knowledge, that he has had opportunities of knowing the truth about this matter; and his judgment, that he has made proper use of those opportunities in coming to the conclusion which he affirms.")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("那么，我们首先要问，在什么情况下，人的见证不值得相信呢？他可能有意或无意地说出不真实的话。在第一种情况下，他在撒谎，这要归咎于他的道德品质；在第二种情况下，他是在撒谎。在第二种情况下，他是无知或错误的，只是他的知识或判断有错误。为了使我们有权接受他的证词作为相信他所说内容的基础，我们必须有合理的理由相信他的真实性，即他确实试图说出他所知道的真相；他的知识，他有机会了解此事的真相；以及他的判断，他已经正确利用了这些机会来得出他所确认的结论。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("However plain and obvious these reasons may be, so that no man of ordinary intelligence, reflecting upon the matter, could fail to arrive at them, it is nevertheless true that a great many persons do habitually disregard them in weighing testimony. Of the two questions, equally important to the trustworthiness of a witness, ‘Is he dishonest?’ and ‘May he be mistaken?’ the majority of mankind are perfectly satisfied if one can, with some show of probability, be answered in the negative. The excellent moral character of a man is alleged as ground for accepting his statements about things which he cannot possibly have known. A Mohammedan, for example, will tell us that the character of his Prophet was so noble and majestic that it commands the reverence even of those who do not believe in his mission. So admirable was his moral teaching, so wisely put together the great social machine which he created, that his precepts have not only been accepted by a great portion of mankind, but have actually been obeyed. His institutions have on the one hand rescued the negro from savagery, and on the other hand have taught civilization to the advancing West; and although the races which held the highest forms of his faith, and most fully embodied his mind and thought, have all been conquered and swept away by barbaric tribes, yet the history of their marvellous attainments remains as an imperishable glory to Islam. Are we to doubt the word of a man so great and so good? Can we suppose that this magnificent genius, this splendid moral hero, has lied to us about the most solemn and sacred matters? The testimony of Mohammed is clear, that there is but one God, and that he, Mohammed, is his prophet; that if we believe in him we shall enjoy everlasting felicity, but that if we do not we shall be damned. This testimony rests on the most awful of foundations, the revelation of heaven itself; for was he not visited by the angel Gabriel, as he fasted and prayed in his desert cave, and allowed to enter into the blessed fields of Paradise? Surely God is God and Mohammed is the Prophet of God.")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("无论这些理由多么简单和明显，任何一个具有普通智力的人在思考这个问题时都无法得出这些理由，但确实有很多人在权衡证词时习惯性地忽视它们。对于证人的可信度同样重要的两个问题是“他是否不诚实？”和“他可能弄错了吗？”如果在某种可能性的情况下得到否定的回答，大多数人都会感到非常满意。一个人的优秀道德品质被认为是接受他对他不可能知道的事情的陈述的基础。例如，一位伊斯兰教徒会告诉我们，他的先知的品格是如此高贵和威严，以至于即使那些不相信他的使命的人也会受到尊敬。他的道德教导是如此令人钦佩，他所创造的伟大社会机器如此明智地组合在一起，以至于他的戒律不仅被大部分人类接受，而且实际上得到了遵守。他的制度一方面将黑人从野蛮中拯救出来，另一方面又向不断进步的西方传授文明。尽管持有他最高形式的信仰、最充分体现他的思想和思想的种族都被野蛮部落征服和消灭了，但他们的非凡成就的历史仍然是伊斯兰教不朽的荣耀。我们难道要怀疑一个如此伟大、如此善良的人的话吗？我们是否可以假设这位伟大的天才，这位出色的道德英雄，在最庄严和神圣的事情上对我们撒了谎？重要吗？穆罕默德的见证很清楚，只有一位神，穆罕默德是他的先知。如果我们相信他，我们将享受永恒的幸福，但如果我们不相信他，我们就会受到诅咒。这个见证建立在最可怕的基础之上，即天上本身的启示。因为当他在沙漠洞穴中禁食和祈祷并被允许进入天堂的祝福田野时，天使加百列不是拜访过他吗？当然，上帝就是上帝，穆罕默德是上帝的先知。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S031")[#text("What should we answer to this Mussulman? First, no doubt, we should be tempted to take exception against his view of the character of the Prophet and the uniformly beneficial influence of Islam: before we could go with him altogether in these matters it might seem that we should have to forget many terrible things of which we have heard or read. But if we chose to grant him all these assumptions, for the sake of argument, and because it is difficult both for the faithful and for infidels to discuss them fairly and without passion, still we should have something to say which takes away the ground of his belief, and therefore shows that it is wrong to entertain it. Namely this: the character of Mohammed is excellent evidence that he was honest and spoke the truth so far as he knew it; but it is no evidence at all that he knew what the truth was. What means could he have of knowing that the form which appeared to him to be the angel Gabriel was not a hallucination, and that his apparent visit to Paradise was not a dream? Grant that he himself was fully persuaded and honestly believed that he had the guidance of heaven, and was the vehicle of a supernatural revelation, how could he know that this strong conviction was not a mistake? Let us put ourselves in his place; we shall find that the more completely we endeavor to realize what passed through his mind, the more clearly we shall perceive that the Prophet could have had no adequate ground for the belief in his own inspiration. It is most probable that he himself never doubted of the matter, or thought of asking the question; but we are in the position of those to whom the question has been asked, and who are bound to answer it. It is known to medical observers that solitude and want of food are powerful means of producing delusion and of fostering a tendency to mental disease. Let us suppose, then, that I, like Mohammed, go into desert places to fast and pray; what things can happen to me which will give me the right to believe that I am divinely inspired? Suppose that I get information, apparently from a celestial visitor, which upon being tested is found to be correct. I cannot be sure, in the first place, that the celestial visitor is not a figment of my own mind, and that the information did not come to me, unknown at the time to my consciousness, through some subtle channel of sense. But if my visitor were a real visitor, and for a long time gave me information which was found to be trustworthy, this would indeed be good ground for trusting him in the future as to such matters as fall within human powers of verification; but it would not be ground for trusting his testimony as to any other matters. For although his tested character would justify me in believing that he spoke the truth so far as he knew, yet the same question would present itself—what ground is there for supposing that he knows?")]

#zh(id: "S031")[#text("我们应该怎样回答这个穆斯林呢？首先，毫无疑问，我们应该对他对先知的品格和伊斯兰教的一致有益影响的看法表示异议：在我们能够在这些问题上完全同意他之前，我们似乎必须忘记我们所听到或读到的许多可怕的事情。但是，如果我们为了争论而选择承认他所有这些假设，并且因为信徒和异教徒都很难公平且不带激情地讨论这些假设，那么我们仍然应该说一些话，从而剥夺了他的信仰的基础，因此表明接受它是错误的。也就是说：穆罕默德的品格很好地证明了他是诚实的，并且据他所知说的是真话；但根本没有证据表明他知道真相是什么。他怎样才能知道他所看到的天使加百列的形象不是幻觉，他表面上的天堂之行也不是一场梦呢？格兰特自己都深信不疑，坚信自己有上天的指引，是超自然启示的载体，怎么知道这种坚定的信念不是错误的呢？让我们设身处地为他着想；我们会发现，我们越是努力彻底了解他的思想，我们就会越清楚地认识到先知不可能有足够的理由相信他自己的灵感。很可能他自己从来没有怀疑过这件事，也没有想过去询问问题；但我们的立场是那些被问到这个问题并且必须回答这个问题的人。医学观察家知道，孤独和缺乏食物是产生妄想和助长精神疾病倾向的有力手段。那么，让我们假设我像穆罕默德一样，前往沙漠地区禁食和祈祷；哪些事情会发生在我身上，让我有权利相信我受到了神圣的启发？假设我得到了信息，显然是来自天体访客，经测试发现该信息是正确的。首先，我无法确定这位天上来客是否是我自己头脑中虚构的，并且这些信息不是通过某种微妙的感觉渠道传到我的，当时我的意识还不知道。但是，如果我的来访者是真正的来访者，并且在很长一段时间内向我提供了值得信赖的信息，那么这确实是我将来在人类验证能力范围内的事情上信任他的良好基础；但这并不能成为相信他对任何其他问题的证词的理由。因为尽管他的性格经受了考验，我相信他所说的是他所知道的真相，但同样的问题也会出现——有什么理由假设他知道呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("Even if my supposed visitor had given me such information, subsequently verified by me, as proved him to have means of knowledge about verifiable matters far exceeding my own; this would not justify me in believing what he said about matters that are not at present capable of verification by man. It would be ground for interesting conjecture, and for the hope that, as the fruit of our patient inquiry, we might by and by attain to such a means of verification as should rightly turn conjecture into belief. For belief belongs to man, and to the guidance of human affairs: no belief is real unless it guide our actions, and those very actions supply a test of its truth.")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("即使我所谓的访客给了我这样的信息，随后由我验证，证明他对可验证问题的了解远远超出了我的知识；这并不能证明我有理由相信他所说的关于目前无法被人类证实的事情。它将为有趣的猜想奠定基础，并希望作为我们耐心探究的成果，我们可能会逐渐获得一种验证手段，从而正确地将猜想转变为信念。因为信仰属于人，属于人类事务的指导：除非它指导我们的行动，否则任何信仰都是真实的，而这些行动恰恰可以检验其真实性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("But, it may be replied, the acceptance of Islam as a system is just that action which is prompted by belief in the mission of the Prophet, and which will serve for a test of its truth. Is it possible to believe that a system which has succeeded so well is really founded upon a delusion? Not only have individual saints found joy and peace in believing, and verified those spiritual experiences which are promised to the faithful, but nations also have been raised from savagery or barbarism to a higher social state. Surely we are at liberty to say that the belief has been acted upon, and that it has been verified.")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("但是，有人可能会回答说，接受伊斯兰教作为一个体系只是出于对先知使命的信仰而采取的行动，这将有助于检验其真理。是否有可能相信一个如此成功的制度实际上是建立在一种错觉之上的？不仅个别圣徒在信仰中找到了喜乐与平安，并证实了那些应许给信徒的精神体验，而且国家也从蒙昧或野蛮状态提升到了更高的社会状态。当然，我们可以自由地说，这个信念已经被付诸行动，并且已经得到验证。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S034")[#text("It requires, however, but little consideration to show that what has really been verified is not at all the supernal character of the Prophet’s mission, or the trustworthiness of his authority in matters which we ourselves cannot test, but only his practical wisdom in certain very mundane things. The fact that believers have found joy and peace in believing gives us the right to say that the doctrine is a comfortable doctrine, and pleasant to the soul; but it does not give us the right to say that it is true. And the question which our conscience is always asking about that which we are tempted to believe is not, ‘Is it comfortable and pleasant?’ but, ‘Is it true?’ That the Prophet preached certain doctrines, and predicted that spiritual comfort would be found in them, proves only his sympathy with human nature and his knowledge of it; but it does not prove his superhuman knowledge of theology.")]

#zh(id: "S034")[#text("然而，无需考虑就能证明，真正被证实的根本不是先知使命的超然特征，也不是他在我们自己无法检验的事情上的权威的可信度，而只是他在某些非常平凡的事情上的实践智慧。信徒在信仰中找到了喜乐与平安，这一事实使我们有权说这个教义是令人舒适的教义，令人愉悦的灵魂；但它并不赋予我们说它是真的的权利。我们的良心总是在问我们想要相信的事情，不是“它舒服吗？令人愉快吗？”而是“这是真的吗？”先知宣扬某些教义，并预言在这些教义中可以找到精神上的安慰，这只能证明他对人性的同情和对人性的了解;但这并不能证明他有超人的神学知识。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S035")[#text("And if we admit for the sake of argument (for it seems that we cannot do more) that the progress made by Moslem nations in certain cases was really due to the system formed and sent forth into the the world by Mohammed, we are not at liberty to conclude from this that he was inspired to declare the truth about things which we cannot verify. We are only at liberty to infer the excellence of his moral precepts, or of the means which he devised for so working upon men as to get them obeyed, or of the social and political machinery which he set up. And it would require a great amount of careful examination into the history of those nations to determine which of these things had the greater share in the result. So that here again it is the Prophet’s knowledge of human nature, and his sympathy with it, that are verified; not his divine inspiration, or his knowledge of theology.")]

#zh(id: "S035")[#text("如果我们为了争论而承认（因为似乎我们不能做得更多）穆斯林国家在某些情况下所取得的进步实际上是由于穆罕默德形成并传播到世界的体系，我们就不能自由地由此得出结论说他受到启发而宣布了我们无法证实的事情的真相。我们只能自由地推断他的道德戒律的卓越性，或者他为使人们服从而设计的手段的卓越性，或者他所建立的社会和政治机制的卓越性。这需要对这些国家的历史进行大量仔细的研究，以确定其中哪一个在结果中所占的份额更大。因此，先知对人性的了解以及他对人性的同情再次得到证实。不是他神圣的灵感，也不是他的神学知识。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S036")[#text("If there were only one Prophet, indeed, it might well seem a difficult and even an ungracious task to decide upon what points we would trust him, and on what we would doubt his authority; seeing what help and furtherance all men have gained in all ages from those who saw more clearly, who felt more strongly, and who sought the truth with more single heart than their weaker brethren. But there is not only one Prophet; and while the consent of many upon that which, as men, they had real means of knowing and did know, has endured to the end, and been honorably built into the great fabric of human knowledge, the diverse witness of some about that which they did not and could not know remains as a warning to us that to exaggerate the prophetic authority is to misuse it, and to dishonor those who have sought only to help and further us after their power. It is hardly in human nature that a man should quite accurately gauge the limits of his own insight; but it is the duty of those who profit by his work to consider carefully where he may have been carried beyond it. If we must needs embalm his possible errors along with his solid achievements, and use his authority as an excuse for believing what he cannot have known, we make of his goodness an occasion to sin.")]

#zh(id: "S036")[#text("事实上，如果只有一位先知，那么决定我们在哪些方面信任他，在哪些方面怀疑他的权威，似乎是一项困难甚至不仁慈的任务。看看各个时代的人们从那些比他们软弱的弟兄看得更清楚、感觉更强烈、用更专一的心寻求真理的人那里获得了多少帮助和促进。但先知不仅只有一位；还有一位先知。虽然许多人对作为人类有真正的了解手段和确实知道的事物的同意一直持续到最后，并光荣地融入到人类知识的伟大结构中，但一些人对他们不知道和不可能知道的事情的不同见证仍然对我们构成警告，夸大预言的权威就是滥用它，并且羞辱那些只寻求帮助和进一步利用他们的力量的人。一个人很难准确地衡量自己洞察力的极限，这不符合人性。但那些从他的工作中获益的人有责任仔细考虑他可能被带到什么地方超越了它。如果我们必须将他可能犯下的错误和他坚实的成就一起保存起来，并利用他的权威作为相信他不可能知道的事情的借口，那么我们就会利用他的善良来犯罪。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S037")[#text("To consider only one other such witness: the followers of the Buddha have at least as much right to appeal to individual and social experience in support of the authority of the Eastern saviour. The special mark of his religion, it is said, that in which it has never been surpassed, is the comfort and consolation which it gives to the sick and sorrowful, the tender sympathy with which it soothes and assuages all the natural griefs of men. And surely no triumph of social morality can be greater or nobler than that which has kept nearly half the human race from persecuting in the name of religion. If we are to trust the accounts of his early followers, he believed himself to have come upon earth with a divine and cosmic mission to set rolling the wheel of the law. Being a prince, he divested himself of his kingdom, and of his free will became acquainted with misery, that he might learn how to meet and subdue it. Could such a man speak falsely about solemn things? And as for his knowledge, was he not a man miraculous with powers more than man’s? He was born of woman without the help of man; he rose into the air and was transfigured before his kinsmen; at last he went up bodily into heaven from the top of Adam’s Peak. Is not his word to be believed in when he testifies of heavenly things?")]

#zh(id: "S037")[#text("仅考虑另一个这样的见证：佛陀的追随者至少有同样的权利诉诸个人和社会经验来支持东方救世主的权威。据说，他的宗教的特殊标志是它给病人和悲伤的人带来安慰和安慰，它用温柔的同情来抚慰和减轻人类所有自然的悲伤。当然，社会道德的胜利没有比使近一半的人类免遭以宗教名义的迫害更伟大或更高尚的了。如果我们相信他早期追随者的说法，他相信自己来到地球是带着神圣和宇宙的使命来推动法律之轮的。作为王子，他放弃了自己的王国，并以自由意志熟悉了苦难，以便学习如何应对和征服它。这样的人能在严肃的事情上说假话吗？至于他的知识，难道不是一个比人类更神奇的人吗？他是在没有男人帮助的情况下由女人所生的；他升到空中，在他的族人面前变了容貌。最后，他的肉身从亚当峰顶升入了天堂。当他见证天上的事时，他的话不是值得相信的吗？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S038")[#text("If there were only he, and no other, with such claims! But there is Mohammed with his testimony; we cannot choose but listen to them both. The Prophet tells us that there is one God, and that we shall live forever in joy or misery, according as we believe in the Prophet or not. The Buddha says that there is no God, and that we shall be annihilated by and by if we are good enough. Both cannot be infallibly inspired; one or the other must have been the victim of a delusion, and thought he knew that which he really did not know. Who shall dare to say which? and how can we justify ourselves in believing that the other was not also deluded?")]

#zh(id: "S038")[#text("如果只有他，没有其他人有这样的主张就好了！但穆罕默德有他的见证；我们别无选择，只能听他们俩的意见。先知告诉我们，只有一位神，无论我们是否相信先知，我们都将永远生活在欢乐或痛苦中。佛陀说，没有上帝，如果我们足够好，我们很快就会被消灭。两者都不能受到无误的启发；其中一人肯定是一种错觉的受害者，以为自己知道他实际上不知道的事情。谁敢说哪个？我们怎样才能证明自己相信对方没有被迷惑呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S039")[#text("We are led, then, to these judgments following. The goodness and greatness of a man do not justify us in accepting a belief upon the warrant of his authority, unless there are reasonable grounds for supposing that he knew the truth of what he was saying. And there can be no grounds for supposing that a man knows that which we, without ceasing to be men, could not be supposed to verify.")]

#zh(id: "S039")[#text("那么，我们就会做出以下判断。一个人的善良和伟大并不能证明我们有理由接受基于他的权威的信仰，除非有合理的理由假设他知道他所说的真相。没有理由认为一个人知道我们在不停止作为人的情况下无法验证的事情。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S040")[#text("If a chemist tells me, who am no chemist, that a certain substance can be made by putting together other substances in certain proportions and subjecting them to a known process, I am quite justified in believing this upon his authority, unless I know anything against his character or his judgment. For his professional training is one which tends to encourage veracity and the honest pursuit of truth, and to produce a dislike of hasty conclusions and slovenly investigation. And I have reasonable ground for supposing that he knows the truth of what he is saying, for although I am no chemist, I can be made to understand so much of the methods and processes of the science as makes it conceivable to me that, without ceasing to be man, I might verify the statement. I may never actually verify it, or even see any experiment which goes toward verifying it; but still I have quite reason enough to justify me in believing that the verification is within the reach of human appliances and powers, and in particular that it has been actually performed by my informant. His result, the belief to which he has been led by his inquiries, is valid not only for himself but for others; it is watched and tested by those who are working in the same ground and who know that no greater service can be rendered to science than the purification of accepted results from the errors which may have crept into them. It is in this way that the result becomes common property, a right object of belief, which is a social affair and matter of public business. Thus it is to be observed that his authority is valid because there are those who question it and verify it; that it is precisely this process of examining and purifying that keeps alive among investigators the love of that which shall stand all possible tests, the sense of public responsibility as of those whose work, if well done, shall remain as the enduring heritage of mankind.")]

#zh(id: "S040")[#text("如果一位化学家告诉我（我不是化学家）某种物质可以通过将其他物质按一定比例放在一起并经过已知的过程而制成，那么我完全有理由相信他的权威，除非我知道任何违背他的性格或判断的事情。因为他所受的专业训练倾向于鼓励诚实和诚实地追求真理，并且不喜欢草率的结论和草率的调查。我有合理的理由假设他知道他所说的真相，因为虽然我不是化学家，但我可以理解科学的方法和过程，这使我可以想象，在不停止作为人的情况下，我可以验证这个陈述。我可能永远不会真正验证它，甚至不会看到任何旨在验证它的实验；但我仍然有足够的理由相信验证是在人类设备和能力的范围内，特别是它实际上是由我的线人进行的。他的结果，即他通过探究而得出的信念，不仅对他自己有效，而且对其他人也有效。它受到那些在同一领域工作的人们的关注和检验，他们知道，没有什么比净化已接受的结果中可能潜入的错误更能为科学提供的服务了。正是通过这种方式，结果才成为公共财产，成为正确的信仰对象，成为社会事务和公共事务。由此可见，他的权威是有效的因为有人质疑，有人验证；正是这种检查和净化的过程，使调查人员保持了对经受一切可能考验的事物的热爱，以及那些工作如果做得好将作为人类不朽遗产的人的公共责任感。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S041")[#text("But if my chemist tells me that an atom of oxygen has existed unaltered in weight and rate of vibration throughout all time, I have no right to believe this on his authority, for it is a thing which he cannot know without ceasing to be man. He may quite honestly believe that this statement is a fair inference from his experiments, but in that case his judgment is at fault. A very simple consideration of the character of experiments would show him that they never can lead to results of such a kind; that being themselves only approximate and limited, they cannot give us knowledge which is exact and universal. No eminence of character and genius can give a man authority enough to justify us in believing him when he makes statements implying exact or universal knowledge.")]

#zh(id: "S041")[#text("但是，如果我的化学家告诉我，氧原子的重量和振动速率自始至终都没有改变过，我就无权相信他的权威，因为如果他不再是人，就无法知道这一点。他可能非常诚实地相信这个陈述是从他的实验中得出的公平推论，但在这种情况下，他的判断是错误的。对实验性质的简单思考就会告诉他，实验永远不可能得出这样的结果。由于它们本身只是近似的和有限的，它们不能给我们精确和普遍的知识。当一个人发表暗示精确或普遍知识的陈述时，任何杰出的品格和天才都不能给予我们足够的权威来证明我们有理由相信他。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S042")[#text("Again, an Arctic explorer may tell us that in a given latitude and longitude he has experienced such and such a degree of cold, that the sea was of such a depth, and the ice of such a character. We should be quite right to believe him, in the absence of any stain upon his veracity. It is conceivable that we might, without ceasing to be men, go there and verify his statement; it can be tested by the witness of his companions, and there is adequate ground for supposing that he knows the truth of what he is saying. But if an old whaler tells us that the ice is three hundred feet thick all the way up to the Pole, we shall not be justified in believing him. For although the statement may be capable of verification by man, it is certainly not capable of verification by him, with any means and appliances which he has possessed; and he must have persuaded himself of the truth of it by some means which does not attach any credit to his testimony. Even if, therefore, the matter affirmed is within the reach of human knowledge, we have no right to accept it upon authority unless it is within the reach of our informant’s knowledge.")]

#zh(id: "S042")[#text("再一次，北极探险家可能会告诉我们，在给定的纬度和经度上，他经历过如此这般的寒冷，海水有如此深，冰的性质如此。我们相信他是完全正确的，因为他的诚实没有任何污点。可以想象，我们仍然可以作为人去那里验证他的说法。这可以通过他的同伴的见证来检验，并且有充分的理由假设他知道他所说的真实性。但如果一位老捕鲸者告诉我们，一直到极地的冰层有三百英尺厚，我们就没有理由相信他。因为虽然这个陈述可能能够被人验证，但他肯定无法用他所拥有的任何手段和设备来验证；他一定是通过某种方法说服自己相信这件事的真实性，但他的证词并没有任何可信度。因此，即使所确认的事项在人类知识范围内，我们也无权根据权威接受它，除非它在我们的线人知识范围内。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S043")[#text("What shall we say of that authority, more venerable and august than any individual witness, the time-honored tradition of the human race? An atmosphere of beliefs and conceptions has been formed by the labors and struggles of our forefathers, which enables us to breathe amid the various and complex circumstances of our life. It is around and about us and within us; we cannot think except in the forms and processes of thought which it supplies. Is it possible to doubt and to test it? and if possible, is it right?")]

#zh(id: "S043")[#text("对于这个比任何个人见证者都更加可敬和威严的权威，即人类历史悠久的传统，我们该怎么说呢？前辈们的劳动和奋斗形成了一种信仰和观念的氛围，使我们能够在复杂多样的生活环境中呼吸。它就在我们周围、关于我们、在我们之内；除了它所提供的思维形式和过程之外，我们无法思考。是否可以怀疑并检验呢？如果可能的话，是吗？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S044")[#text("We shall find reason to answer that it is not only possible and right, but our bounden duty; that the main purpose of the tradition itself is to supply us with the means of asking questions, of testing and inquiring into things; that if we misuse it, and take it as a collection of cut-and-dried statements, to be accepted without further inquiry, we are not only injuring ourselves here, but by refusing to do our part toward the building up of the fabric which shall be inherited by our children, we are tending to cut off ourselves and our race from the human line.")]

#zh(id: "S044")[#text("我们会找到理由回答，这不仅是可能的、正确的，而且是我们义不容辞的责任；传统本身的主要目的是为我们提供提出问题、测试和探究事物的手段；如果我们误用它，把它当作一套现成的陈述，不经进一步询问就接受，我们不仅会在这里伤害自己，而且拒绝为建立我们的子孙后代的结构尽自己的一份力量，我们往往会切断我们自己和我们的种族与人类血统的联系。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S045")[#text("Let us first take care to distinguish a kind of tradition which especially requires to be examined and called in question, because it especially shrinks from inquiry. Suppose that a medicine-man in Central Africa tells his tribe that a certain powerful medicine in his tent will be propitiated if they kill their cattle; and that the tribe believe him. Whether the medicine was propitiated or not, there are no means of verifying, but the cattle are gone. Still the belief may be kept up in the tribe that propitiation has been effected in this way; and in a later generation it will be all the easier for another medicine-man to persuade them to a similar act. Here the only reason for belief is that everybody has believed the thing for so long that it must be true. And yet the belief was founded on fraud, and has been propagated by credulity. That man will undoubtedly do right, and be a friend of men who shall call it in question and see that there is no evidence for it, help his neighbors to see as he does, and even, if need be, go into the holy tent and break the medicine.")]

#zh(id: "S045")[#text("让我们首先注意区分一种特别需要审视和质疑的传统，因为它特别回避探究。假设中非的一位巫师告诉他的部落，如果他们杀了他们的牛，他的帐篷里有某种强力药物会得到安抚；并且部落相信他。药有没有安抚，无从考证，但牛已经走了。尽管如此，部落中仍然相信安抚是通过这种方式实现的。在下一代人中，另一位巫师会更容易说服他们采取类似的行动。在这里，相信的唯一理由是每个人都相信这件事太久了，它一定是真的。然而，这种信念是建立在欺诈之上的，并且是通过轻信而传播的。毫无疑问，这个人会做正确的事，成为人们的朋友，他们会提出质疑，看看没有证据证明这一点，帮助他的邻居看到他所做的，甚至，如果需要的话，进入神圣的帐篷，打破药物。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S046")[#text("The rule, which should guide us in such cases is simple and obvious enough: that the aggregate testimony of our neighbors is subject to the same conditions as the testimony of any one of them. Namely, we have no right to believe a thing true because everybody says so, unless there are good grounds for believing that some one person at least has the means of knowing what is true, and is speaking the truth so far as he knows it. However many nations and generations of men are brought into the witness-box, they cannot testify to anything which they do not know. Every man who has accepted the statement from somebody else, without himself testing and verifying it, is out of court; his word is worth nothing at all. And when we get back at last to the true birth and beginning of the statement, two serious questions must be disposed of in regard to him who first made it: was he mistaken in thinking that he knew about this matter, or was he lying?")]

#zh(id: "S046")[#text("在这种情况下应该指导我们的规则是简单而明显的：我们邻居的集体证词与他们中任何一个人的证词都受到相同的条件的约束。也就是说，我们没有权利因为每个人都这么说而相信一件事是真的，除非有充分的理由相信某人至少有办法知道什么是真实的，并且所说的是他所知道的真相。无论多少个国家、多少代人被带上证人席，他们都无法证明任何他们不知道的事情。任何未经本人检验和核实而接受他人陈述的人都将被出庭。他的话毫无价值。当我们最终回到这个陈述的真正诞生和开始时，对于第一个提出这个陈述的人来说，必须解决两个严重的问题：他是否错误地认为他知道这件事，或者他在撒谎？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S047")[#text("This last question is unfortunately a very actual and practical one even to us at this day and in this country. We have no occasion to go to La Salette, or to Central Africa, or to Lourdes, for examples of immoral and debasing superstition. It is only too possible for a child to grow up in London surrounded by an atmosphere of beliefs fit only for the savage, which have in our own time been founded in fraud and propagated by credulity.")]

#zh(id: "S047")[#text("不幸的是，最后一个问题即使对我们今天和这个国家来说也是一个非常现实和实际的问题。我们没有机会去拉萨莱特、中非或卢尔德寻找不道德和可耻的迷信的例子。一个孩子很有可能在伦敦长大，周围充满了只适合野蛮人的信仰氛围，而在我们这个时代，这些信仰是建立在欺诈之上并通过轻信传播的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S048")[#text("Laying aside, then, such tradition as is handed on without testing by successive generations, let us consider that which is truly built up out of the common experience of mankind. This great fabric is for the guidance of our thoughts, and through them of our actions, both in the moral and in the material world. In the moral world, for example, it gives us the conceptions of right in general, of justice, of truth, of beneficence, and the like. These are given as conceptions, not as statements or propositions; they answer to certain definite instincts, which are certainly within us, however they came there. That it is right to be beneficent is matter of immediate personal experience; for when a man retires within himself and there finds something, wider and more lasting than his solitary personality which says, ‘I want to do right,’ as well as, ‘I want to do good to man,’ he can verify by direct observation that one instinct is founded upon and agrees fully with the other. And it is his duty so to verify this and all similar statements.")]

#zh(id: "S048")[#text("那么，我们先把那些未经后代检验而传承下来的传统放在一边，让我们考虑一下真正从人类共同经验中建立起来的传统。这个伟大的结构可以指导我们的思想，并通过它们指导我们的行动，无论是在道德世界还是在物质世界。例如，在道德世界中，它为我们提供了一般权利、正义、真理、仁慈等概念。这些是以概念的形式给出的，而不是以陈述或命题的形式给出的；它们回应了某些明确的本能，无论它们是如何出现的，这些本能肯定存在于我们体内。行善是正确的，这是个人的直接经验；因为当一个人在自己内心隐退，并找到比他孤独的人格更广泛、更持久的东西时，它说：“我想做正确的事”，以及“我想为人类做好事”，他可以通过直接观察来验证一种本能是建立在另一种本能的基础上并与另一种本能完全一致的。他有责任核实这一声明以及所有类似的声明。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S049")[#text("The tradition says also, at a definite place and time, that such and such actions are just, or true, or beneficent. For all such rules a further inquiry is necessary, since they are sometimes established by an authority other than that of the moral sense founded on experience. Until recently, the moral tradition of our own country—and indeed of all Europe—taught that it was beneficent to give money indiscriminately to beggars. But the questioning of this rule, and investigation into it, led men to see that true beneficence is that which helps a man to do the work which he is most fitted for, not that which keeps and encourages him in idleness; and that to neglect this distinction in the present is to prepare pauperism and misery for the future. By this testing and discussion, not only has practice been purified and made more beneficent, but the very conception of beneficence has been made wider and wiser. Now here the great social heirloom consists of two parts: the instinct of beneficence, which makes a certain side of our nature, when predominant, wish to do good to men; and the intellectual conception of beneficence, which we can compare with any proposed course of conduct and ask, ‘Is this beneficent or not?’ By the continual asking and answering of such questions the conception grows in breadth and distinctness, and the instinct becomes strengthened and purified. It appears then that the great use of the conception, the intellectual part of the heirloom, is to enable us to ask questions; that it grows and is kept straight by means of these questions; and if we do not use it for that purpose we shall gradually lose it altogether, and be left with a mere code of regulations which cannot rightly be called morality at all.")]

#zh(id: "S049")[#text("传统还说，在特定的地点和时间，这样那样的行为是正义的、真实的或仁慈的。对于所有这些规则，进一步的探究是必要的，因为它们有时是由权威而不是基于经验的道德感建立的。直到最近，我们国家乃至整个欧洲的道德传统都告诉我们，不分青红皂白地向乞丐施舍是仁慈的。但是，对这一规则的质疑和研究使人们认识到，真正的仁慈是帮助一个人做他最适合做的工作，而不是让他保持和鼓励他无所事事；现在忽视这种区别就是为未来的贫困和痛苦做好准备。通过这样的检验和讨论，不仅修行得到了净化和变得更加仁慈，而且仁慈的概念本身也变得更加广泛和明智。这里，伟大的社会传家宝由两部分组成：行善的本能，这使得我们天性的某一方面占主导地位时，希望为人类做好事；以及仁慈的理智概念，我们可以将其与任何拟议的行为方针进行比较，并问：“这是否仁慈？”通过不断地提出和回答这些问题，概念的广度和清晰度不断增长，本能也得到加强和净化。看来，这个概念（传家宝的智力部分）的巨大用途是使我们能够提出问题；通过这些问题，它得以成长并保持笔直；如果我们不这样做如果我们用它来达到这个目的，我们将逐渐完全失去它，只剩下一套根本不能正确称为道德的规章制度。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S050")[#text("Such considerations apply even more obviously and clearly, if possible, to the store of beliefs and conceptions which our fathers have amassed for us in respect of the material world. We are ready to laugh at the rule of thumb of the Australian, who continues to tie his hatchet to the side of the handle, although the Birmingham fitter has made a hole on purpose for him to put the handle in. His people have tied up hatchets so for ages: who is he that he should set himself up against their wisdom? He has sunk so low that he cannot do what some of them must have done in the far distant past—call in question an established usage, and invent or learn something better. Yet here, in the dim beginning of knowledge, where science and art are one, we find only the same simple rule which applies to the highest and deepest growths of that cosmic Tree; to its loftiest flower-tipped branches as well as to the profoundest of its hidden roots; the rule, namely, that what is stored up and handed down to us is rightly used by those who act as the makers acted, when they stored it up; those who use it to ask further questions, to examine, to investigate; who try honestly and solemnly to find out what is the right way of looking at things and of dealing with them.")]

#zh(id: "S050")[#text("如果可能的话，这些考虑甚至更明显和明确地适用于我们的祖先为我们积累的关于物质世界的信念和观念。我们已经准备好嘲笑澳大利亚人的经验法则了，他继续将斧头绑在手柄的侧面，尽管伯明翰的装配工故意挖了一个洞让他把手柄放进去。他的人民多年来一直把斧头绑起来：他是谁，竟然要与他们的智慧作对？他已经堕落得如此之低，以至于他无法做到他们中的一些人在遥远的过去一定做过的事情——质疑既定的用法，并发明或学习更好的东西。然而在这里，在知识的朦胧开端，科学与艺术合而为一，我们只发现同样的简单规则适用于那棵宇宙之树的最高和最深的生长；到它最高的花尖枝，到它最深的隐藏根；规则，即，储存并传给我们的东西，被那些在储存它时按照制造者的行为正确使用的人；那些用它来提出进一步问题、检查、调查的人；他们诚实而严肃地尝试找出看待事物和处理事物的正确方式。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S051")[#text("A question rightly asked is already half answered, said Jacobi; we may add that the method of solution is the other half of the answer, and that the actual result counts for nothing by the side of these two. For an example let us go to the telegraph, where theory and practice, grown each to years of discretion, are marvelously wedded for the fruitful service of men. Ohm found that the strength of an electric current is directly proportional to the strength of the battery which produces it, and inversely as the length of the wire along which it has to travel. This is called Ohm’s law; but the result, regarded as a statement to be believed, is not the valuable part of it. The first half is the question: what relation holds good between these quantities? So put, the question involves already the conception of strength of current, and of strength of battery, as quantities to be measured and compared; it hints clearly that these are the things to be attended to in the study of electric currents. The second half is the method of investigation; how to measure these quantities, what instruments are required for the experiment, and how are they to be used? The student who begins to learn about electricity is not asked to believe in Ohm’s law: he is made to understand the question, he is placed before the apparatus, and he is taught to verify it. He learns to do things, not to think he knows things; to use instruments and to ask questions, not to accept a traditional statement. The question which required a genius to ask it rightly is answered by a tyro. If Ohm’s law were suddenly lost and forgotten by all men, while the question and the method of solution remained, the result could be rediscovered in an hour. But the result by itself, if known to a people who could not comprehend the value of the question or the means of solving it, would be like a watch in the hands of a savage who could not wind it up, or an iron steam-ship worked by Spanish engineers.")]

#zh(id: "S051")[#text("雅可比说，正确提出的问题已经得到了一半的回答；我们可以补充一点，解决方法是答案的另一半，实际结果与这两者相比毫无意义。让我们以电报为例，理论和实践经过多年的谨慎发展，奇妙地结合在一起，为人类提供了卓有成效的服务。欧姆发现电流的强度与产生电流的电池的强度成正比，与电流传输的电线的长度成反比。这就是所谓的欧姆定律；但结果，被视为值得相信的陈述，并不是其有价值的部分。前半部分的问题是：这些量之间有什么关系？因此，这个问题已经涉及电流强度和电池强度的概念，作为要测量和比较的量；它清楚地暗示了这些是电流研究中需要注意的事情。下半部分是调查方法；如何测量这些量，实验需要什么仪器，以及如何使用它们？开始学习电学的学生并不被要求相信欧姆定律：他被要求理解这个问题，他被放在仪器前，并被教导如何验证它。他学会做事，而不是认为自己知道事；使用工具并提出问题，而不是接受传统的陈述。需要天才才能正确提出的问题却由新手回答。如果欧姆定律突然被所有人迷失和遗忘，虽然问题和解决方法仍然存在，但结果可以在一个小时内重新发现。但结果本身，如果一个无法理解问题的价值或解决问题的方法的人知道的话，就会像一个无法上发条的野蛮人手中的手表，或者西班牙工程师制造的一艘铁轮船。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S052")[#text("In regard, then, to the sacred tradition of humanity, we learn that it consists, not in propositions or statements which are to be accepted and believed on the authority of the tradition, but in questions rightly asked, in conceptions which enable us to ask further questions, and in methods of answering questions. The value of all these things depends on their being tested day by day. The very sacredness of the precious deposit imposes upon us the duty and the responsibility of testing it, of purifying and enlarging it to the utmost of our power. He who makes use of its results to stifle his own doubts, or to hamper the inquiry of others, is guilty of a sacrilege which centuries shall never be able to blot out. When the labors and questionings of honest and brave men shall have built up the fabric of known truth to a glory which we in this generation can neither hope for nor imagine, in that pure and holy temple he shall have no part nor lot, but his name and his works shall be cast out into the darkness of oblivion forever.")]

#zh(id: "S052")[#text("那么，就人类的神圣传统而言，我们了解到，它并不在于根据传统的权威而被接受和相信的命题或陈述，而是在于正确提出的问题，在于使我们能够提出进一步问题的概念，以及在于回答问题的方法。所有这些东西的价值取决于它们日复一日的考验。珍贵矿藏的神圣性赋予我们检验它、尽最大努力净化和扩大它的义务和责任。任何人利用其结果来压制自己的怀疑，或阻碍他人的探究，就犯下了几个世纪都无法抹去的亵渎罪。当诚实而勇敢的人的劳动和质疑将已知真理的结构建立到我们这一代人既无法希望也无法想象的荣耀时，在那座纯洁而神圣的圣殿中，他将不再拥有任何地位，但他的名字和他的作品将被永远扔进遗忘的黑暗中。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S053")[#text("III. The Limits of Inference.—The question in what cases we may believe that which goes beyond our experience, is a very large and delicate one, extending to the whole range of scientific method, and requiring a considerable increase in the application of it before it can be answered with anything approaching to completeness. But one rule, lying on the threshold of the subject, of extreme simplicity and vast practical importance, may here be touched upon and shortly laid down.")]

#zh(id: "S053")[#text("三．推论的局限性——在什么情况下我们可以相信超出我们经验的东西是一个非常大而微妙的问题，它延伸到科学方法的整个范围，并且需要大量增加它的应用才能得到接近完整的答案。但是，在这个主题的门槛上，有一条极其简单且具有巨大实际重要性的规则，可以在这里触及并很快制定出来。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S054")[#text("A little reflection will show us that every belief, even the simplest and most fundamental, goes beyond experience when regarded as a guide to our actions. A burnt child dreads the fire, because it believes that the fire will burn it to-day just as it did yesterday; but this belief goes beyond experience, and assumes that the unknown fire of to-day is like the known fire of yesterday. Even the belief that the child was burnt yesterday goes beyond present experience, which contains only the memory of a burning, and not the burning itself; it assumes, therefore, that this memory is trustworthy, although we know that a memory may often be mistaken. But if it is to be used as a guide to action, as a hint of what the future is to be, it must assume something about that future, namely, that it will be consistent with the supposition that the burning really took place yesterday; which is going beyond experience. Even the fundamental ‘I am,’ which cannot be doubted, is no guide to action until it takes to itself ‘I shall be,’ which goes beyond experience. The question is not, therefore, ‘May we believe what goes beyond experience?’ for this is involved in the very nature of belief; but ‘How far and in what manner may we add to our experience in forming our beliefs?’")]

#zh(id: "S054")[#text("稍加反思就会告诉我们，每一个信念，即使是最简单和最基本的信念，当被视为我们行动的指南时，都超越了经验。被烧伤的孩子害怕火，因为他相信火今天会像昨天一样烧伤他；但这种信念超越了经验，并假设今天的未知火灾就像昨天已知的火灾一样。甚至连孩子昨天被烧伤的信念也超越了目前的经验，目前的经验只包含烧伤的记忆，而不包含烧伤本身；因此，它假定这种记忆是值得信赖的，尽管我们知道记忆可能经常是错误的。但如果它要被用作行动指南，作为未来的暗示，它就必须对未来做出一些假设，即它将与昨天确实发生了燃烧的假设相一致；这超越了经验。即使是不容置疑的基本的“我是”，也不能成为行动的指南，除非它本身就超越了经验的“我将是”。因此，问题不是“我们可以相信超越经验的事物吗？”，因为这涉及信仰的本质；而是“我们是否可以相信超越经验的事物？”。而是“我们在形成信念的过程中可以在多大程度上以及以何种方式增加我们的经验？”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S055")[#text("And an answer, of utter simplicity and universality, is suggested by the example we have taken: a burnt child dreads the fire. We may go beyond experience by assuming that what we do not know is like what we do know; or, in other words, we may add to our experience on the assumption of a uniformity in nature. What this uniformity precisely is, how we grow in the knowledge of it from generation to generation, these are questions which for the present we lay aside, being content to examine two instances which may serve to make plainer the nature of the rule.")]

#zh(id: "S055")[#text("我们举的例子给出了一个极其简单和普遍的答案：被烧伤的孩子害怕火。我们可能会超越经验，假设我们不知道的事情就像我们所知道的一样；或者，换句话说，我们可以在假设性质一致的情况下增加我们的经验。这种统一性到底是什么，我们如何一代一代地增长对它的认识，这些问题我们暂时搁置一边，满足于考察两个可能有助于使规则的本质更加清晰的例子。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S056")[#text("From certain observations made with the spectroscope, we infer the existence of hydrogen in the sun. By looking into the spectroscope when the sun is shining on its slit, we see certain definite bright lines: and experiments made upon bodies on the earth have taught us that when these bright lines are seen hydrogen is the source of them. We assume, then, that the unknown bright lines in the sun are like the known bright lines of the laboratory, and that hydrogen in the sun behaves as hydrogen under similar circumstances would behave on the earth.")]

#zh(id: "S056")[#text("根据分光镜的某些观察，我们推断太阳中存在氢。当太阳照射在分光镜的狭缝上时，通过观察分光镜，我们会看到某些明确的亮线：对地球上的物体进行的实验告诉我们，当看到这些亮线时，氢就是它们的来源。那么，我们假设太阳中未知的亮线就像实验室中已知的亮线一样，并且太阳中的氢的行为与类似情况下氢在地球上的行为相同。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S057")[#text("But are we not trusting our spectroscope too much? Surely, having found it to be trustworthy for terrestrial substances, where its statements can be verified by man, we are justified in accepting its testimony in other like cases; but not when it gives us information about things in the sun, where its testimony cannot be directly verified by man?")]

#zh(id: "S057")[#text("但我们是不是太相信我们的光谱仪了？当然，既然发现它对于陆地物质来说是值得信赖的，它的陈述可以由人类验证，我们就有理由接受它在其他类似情况下的证词；但当它为我们提供有关阳光下事物的信息时，它的证词无法直接被人类验证，那又如何呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S058")[#text("Certainly, we want to know a little more before this inference can be justified; and fortunately we do know this. The spectroscope testifies to exactly the same thing in the two cases; namely, that light-vibrations of a certain rate are being sent through it. Its construction is such that if it were wrong about this in one case, it would be wrong in the other. When we come to look into the matter, we find that we have really assumed the matter of the sun to be like the matter of the earth, made up of a certain number of distinct substances; and that each of these, when very hot, has a distinct rate of vibration, by which it may be recognized and singled out from the rest. But this is the kind of assumption which we are justified in using when we add to our experience. It is an assumption of uniformity in nature, and can only be checked by comparison with many similar assumptions which we have to make in other such cases.")]

#zh(id: "S058")[#text("当然，在这个推论得到证实之前，我们想知道更多一点。幸运的是我们确实知道这一点。分光镜在这两种情况下证明了完全相同的事情；也就是说，一定速率的光振动通过它发送。它的结构是这样的，如果在一种情况下它是错误的，那么在另一种情况下它也会是错误的。当我们研究这个物质时，我们发现我们确实假设太阳的物质和地球的物质一样，都是由一定数量的不同物质组成的；并且，当非常热时，它们中的每一个都具有不同的振动速率，通过它可以将其识别出来并从其余的中挑选出来。但当我们增加我们的经验时，我们有理由使用这种假设。这是本质上一致的假设，只能通过与我们在其他此类情况下做出的许多类似假设进行比较来检验。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S059")[#text("But is this a true belief, of the existence of hydrogen in the sun? Can it help in the right guidance of human action?")]

#zh(id: "S059")[#text("但这是太阳中存在氢的真实信念吗？它能帮助正确指导人类行动吗？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S060")[#text("Certainly not, if it is accepted on unworthy grounds, and without some understanding of the process by which it is got at. But when this process is taken in as the ground of the belief, it becomes a very serious and practical matter. For if there is no hydrogen in the sun, the spectroscope—that is to say, the measurement of rates of vibration—must be an uncertain guide in recognizing different substances; and consequently it ought not to be used in chemical analysis—in assaying, for example—to the great saving of time, trouble, and money. Whereas the acceptance of the spectroscopic method as trustworthy, has enriched us not only with new metals, which is a great thing, but with new processes of investigation, which is vastly greater.")]

#zh(id: "S060")[#text("当然不会，如果它以不值得的理由被接受，并且对它的实现过程没有一定的了解。但当这个过程被当作信仰的基础时，它就变成了一件非常严肃和实际的事情。因为如果太阳中没有氢，那么分光镜——也就是说，振动速率的测量——在识别不同物质时一定是一个不确定的指南；因此，它不应该用于化学分析（例如测定），这样可以大大节省时间、麻烦和金钱。然而，人们接受光谱方法是值得信赖的，这不仅使我们丰富了新金属（这是一件伟大的事情），而且还使我们丰富了新的研究过程，这要大得多。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S061")[#text("For another example, let us consider the way in which we infer the truth of an historical event—say the siege of Syracuse in the Peloponnesian war. Our experience is that manuscripts exist which are said to be and which call themselves manuscripts of the history of Thucydides; that in other manuscripts, stated to be by later historians, he is described as living during the time of the war; and that books, supposed to date from the revival of learning, tell us how these manuscripts had been preserved and were then acquired. We find also that men do not, as a rule, forge books and histories without a special motive; we assume that in this respect men in the past were like men in the present; and we observe that in this case no special motive was present. That is, we add to our experience on the assumption of a uniformity in the characters of men. Because our knowledge of this uniformity is far less complete and exact than our knowledge of that which obtains in physics, inferences of the historical kind are more precarious and less exact than inferences in many other sciences.")]

#zh(id: "S061")[#text("再举个例子，让我们考虑一下我们推断历史事件真相的方式——比如伯罗奔尼撒战争中对锡拉丘兹的围攻。我们的经验是，存在据说是修昔底德历史手稿的手稿。后来的历史学家称，在其他手稿中，他被描述为生活在战争时期；那些被认为是学术复兴时期的书籍告诉我们这些手稿是如何保存并随后获得的。我们还发现，一般来说，人们不会在没有特殊动机的情况下伪造书籍和历史。我们假设，在这方面，过去的人与现在的人是一样的。我们观察到，在这种情况下，并没有特殊的动机。也就是说，我们在假设人类性格具有一致性的基础上增加了我们的经验。因为我们对这种一致性的了解远不如我们在物理学中获得的知识完整和准确，所以历史类型的推论比许多其他科学中的推论更加不稳定和不准确。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S062")[#text("But if there is any special reason to suspect the character of the persons who wrote or transmitted certain books, the case becomes altered. If a group of documents give internal evidence that they were produced among people who forged books in the names of others, and who, in describing events, suppressed those things which did not suit them, while they amplified such as did suit them; who not only committed these crimes, but gloried in them as proofs of humility and zeal; then we must say that upon such documents no true historical inference can be founded, but only unsatisfactory conjecture.")]

#zh(id: "S062")[#text("但如果有任何特殊理由怀疑某些书籍的作者或传播者的品格，情况就会发生变化。如果一组文件提供了内部证据，证明它们是由那些以他人名义伪造书籍的人制作的，这些人在描述事件时压制了那些不适合他们的东西，同时放大了适合他们的东西；他们不仅犯下了这些罪行，还以此为荣，以此作为谦卑和热心的证明；那么我们必须说，根据这些文献并不能建立真正的历史推论，而只能是令人不满意的推测。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S063")[#text("We may, then, add to our experience on the assumption of a uniformity in nature; we may fill in our picture of what is and has been, as experience gives it us, in such a way as to make the whole consistent with this uniformity. And practically demonstrative inference—that which gives us a right to believe in the result of it—is a clear showing that in no other way than by the truth of this result can the uniformity of nature be saved.")]

#zh(id: "S063")[#text("那么，我们可以在假设性质一致的基础上增加我们的经验；我们可以按照经验所给我们的那样，以一种使整体与这种一致性相一致的方式来填充我们对现在和过去的情况的描述。而实际上的证明性推论——赋予我们相信其结果的权利——清楚地表明，除了这个结果的真实性之外，没有其他方法可以拯救自然的统一性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S064")[#text("No evidence, therefore, can justify us in believing the truth of a statement which is contrary to, or outside of, the uniformity of nature. If our experience is such that it cannot be filled up consistently with uniformity, all we have a right to conclude is that there is something wrong somewhere; but the possibility of inference is taken away; we must rest in our experience, and not go beyond it at all. If an event really happened which was not a part of the uniformity of nature, it would have two properties: no evidence could give the right to believe it to any except those whose actual experience it was; and no inference worthy of belief could be founded upon it at all.")]

#zh(id: "S064")[#text("因此，没有任何证据可以证明我们有理由相信违背自然统一性或不符合自然统一性的陈述的真实性。如果我们的经验不能始终如一地充满，我们就有权得出结论，那就是某个地方出了问题；但推理的可能性被剥夺了；我们必须安于我们的经验，而不是完全超越它。如果一个事件确实发生了，但它不属于自然统一性的一部分，它就会有两个属性：除了那些实际经历过的人之外，没有任何证据可以赋予任何人相信它的权利；在此基础上根本无法做出任何值得相信的推论。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S065")[#text("Are we then bound to believe that nature is absolutely and universally uniform? Certainly not; we have no right to believe anything of this kind. The rule only tells us that in forming beliefs which go beyond our experience, we may make the assumption that nature is practically uniform so far as we are concerned. Within the range of human action and verification, we may form, by help of this assumption, actual beliefs; beyond it, only those hypotheses which serve for the more accurate asking of questions.")]

#zh(id: "S065")[#text("那么我们是否一定要相信自然是绝对且普遍统一的呢？当然不是；我们没有权利相信任何此类事情。该规则只是告诉我们，在形成超出我们经验的信念时，我们可能会假设自然就我们而言实际上是统一的。在人类行动和验证的范围内，我们可以借助这个假设形成实际的信念；除此之外，只有那些有助于更准确地提出问题的假设。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S066")[#text("To sum up:—")]

#zh(id: "S066")[#text("总结一下：——")]

#html.p(id: "en-S067")[#text("We may believe what goes beyond our experience, only when it is inferred from that experience by the assumption that what we do not know is like what we know.")]

#zh(id: "S067")[#text("只有当我们假设我们所不知道的事情就像我们所知道的那样，从经验中推断出来时，我们才可能相信超出我们经验的事情。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S068")[#text("We may believe the statement of another person, when there is reasonable ground for supposing that he knows the matter of which he speaks, and that he is speaking the truth so far as he knows it.")]

#zh(id: "S068")[#text("当有合理的理由假设另一个人知道他所说的事情并且他所说的是他所知道的真相时，我们可以相信另一个人的陈述。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S069")[#text("It is wrong in all cases to believe on insufficient evidence; and where it is presumption to doubt and to investigate, there it is worse than presumption to believe.")]

#zh(id: "S069")[#text("在任何情况下，根据证据不足而相信都是错误的；怀疑和调查是假定的，而相信则比假定更糟糕。")]
